[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":123},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1795653645000,"id","qbjpl6k39dav3jyaiaqdrl1cz","title","AWS CloudHSM 迁移至 AWS KMS 的迁移指南：零停机、成本与密钥管理","originalTitle","Reduce operational overhead by migrating from AWS CloudHSM to AWS KMS","summary","AWS CloudHSM 迁移至 AWS KMS 可减少 HSM 设备配置、补丁和高可用性维护开销，多数工作负载可通过改造应用、导入或轮换密钥并重加密数据逐步迁移。迁移可零停机进行，但传统 PKCS#11 等接口需重构，部分支付场景可使用 AWS Payment Cryptography；导入的非对称密钥还应通过 multi-Region key 和异地备份保障可用性。","reason","source",{"_72":122},"links",{"_61":121},"publishedAt","2026-11-25T19:54:52.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-25T20:27:23.504Z","timelineAt","category","blue-team","tags",[116,117,118,119,120],"score",21,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_79":80,"_13":81},"x","readingMode","full","author","Derek Tumulak","body",{"_112":-5,"_61":113,"_114":-5,"_115":36},"outline",[82,83,84,85,86,87,88,89,90],"relatedStories",[78],"topics",[68,69,70],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","fwZ8y4vQhw40","reportCount",1,"additionalSourceCount",0,{"_71":76,"_72":77},{"_71":74,"_72":75},{"_71":30,"_72":73},"slug","name","蓝队与运营","crypto","密码学","cloud-native","云与原生",{"_79":80,"_13":81},"publicId","5ba0f4db-39f0-45f8-9cc1-87c8ddfbeb87","AWS发布CloudHSM迁移KMS指南",{"_11":110,"_92":111,"_93":97},{"_11":108,"_92":109,"_93":97},{"_11":106,"_92":107,"_93":94},{"_11":104,"_92":105,"_93":94},{"_11":102,"_92":103,"_93":97},{"_11":100,"_92":101,"_93":97},{"_11":98,"_92":99,"_93":97},{"_11":95,"_92":96,"_93":97},{"_11":91,"_92":45,"_93":94},"sec-9","text","level",3,"sec-8","Conclusion",2,"sec-7","Next steps","sec-6","Recommendations","sec-5","Migration results","sec-4","Encryption workloads","sec-3","Digital signature workloads","sec-2","How migration works","sec-1","Is migration right for you?","zh","<p><span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kms\">AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)</a></span> supports the security certifications, algorithms, and performance that most <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/cloudhsm\">AWS CloudHSM</a></span> workloads require. Organizations that migrate from AWS CloudHSM to AWS KMS have significantly reduced costs while eliminating hardware security module (HSM) provisioning, patching, and high-availability management. For a detailed comparison of both services, see <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/aws-kms-or-aws-cloudhsm-choose-the-right-key-management-solution/\">AWS KMS or AWS CloudHSM: Choose the right key management solution</a></span>. For payment workloads, see <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/payment-cryptography/\"><u>AWS Payment Cryptography</u></a></span> in the following table.</p><h2 id=\"sec-1\">Is migration right for you?</h2><p>Most AWS CloudHSM workloads can migrate to AWS KMS. The following requirements might appear to need CloudHSM, but most have a resolution path:</p><table> \n    <tbody> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Potential requirement</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Resolution</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Contractual or compliance mandate requiring single-tenant HSMs</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Review your compliance documentation for explicit single-tenant language; many organizations find that it’s assumed, not mandated. Where mandated, a security review of AWS KMS typically confirms that its logical cryptographic isolation architecture satisfies the underlying intent.</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Applications that rely on traditional HSM interfaces (PKCS#11, Java Cryptography Extension, OpenSSL Provider, or Key Storage Provider)</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">These interfaces are a communication pattern, not an architectural constraint. Contact your AWS account team to assess the refactoring effort needed for your workloads to use modern RESTful interfaces to web services.</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Deprecated algorithms (TDES, PKCS#1 v1.5 with RSA) or less common operations (AES key wrapping, AES-CTR, AES-CBC)</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Evaluate whether you can retire the dependency as part of your security modernization.</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Over 1 billion cryptographic operations per month, where higher per-operation AWS KMS costs might favor the per-hour AWS CloudHSM cost model</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Per-operation pricing is only one of several factors that affect cost. Also consider saved staff time, patching, and high-availability management. With envelope encryption (Option B in the following section), optional data key caching can further reduce API call volume.</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Payment workloads using older or payment-specific cryptography (TDES, AES-CBC)</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">While AWS KMS embeds cryptographic best practices in a managed service, some payment systems still rely on older cryptography such as TDES. Evaluate Payment Cryptography, which supports payment use cases, including TDES-based algorithms such as CVV2, ARQC, and PIN validation. It also provides low-level algorithms, such as AES-CBC, that third-party systems might require for interoperability.</td> \n     </tr> \n    </tbody> \n   </table><p>If you can resolve these requirements, it’s possible to migrate from AWS CloudHSM to AWS KMS. You can use the rest of this post to review the basic steps of migration to AWS KMS.</p><h2 id=\"sec-2\">How migration works</h2><p>Migration has three parts: updating application code to call KMS APIs, rotating or importing your cryptographic keys, and transitioning existing data. You re-encrypt previously encrypted data under KMS keys, and existing signatures remain verifiable without AWS CloudHSM. You can complete each part gradually, with zero downtime. Application refactoring is the bulk of the effort, though AI-assisted code transformation tools can reduce this work. Because you’re already refactoring application code, this migration is also an opportunity to adopt post-quantum cryptography (PQC) algorithms, discussed in the following section.</p><h3 id=\"sec-3\">Digital signature workloads</h3><p>During the transition, the AWS CloudHSM private key continues to sign existing workloads. If AWS CloudHSM lets you export (wrap) a copy of your private key, import it directly into AWS KMS to maintain continuity. For details, see <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-migrate-asymmetric-keys-from-cloudhsm-to-aws-kms/\">How to Migrate Asymmetric Keys from CloudHSM to AWS KMS</a></span>. If the key is non-exportable, create a new key pair in AWS KMS. A new asymmetric KMS key (or the imported key from CloudHSM) signs new data flowing to AWS KMS. Shift traffic from AWS CloudHSM to AWS KMS at your own pace. If you created a new key pair, export the CloudHSM public key before decommissioning and use it to verify existing signatures locally with libraries such as AWS-LC or OpenSSL as shown in Figure 1. AWS KMS doesn’t support importing public keys.</p><p>\n   <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/d0dc5fd1d568c66c.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/e751755712ace320.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/f708903804df4017.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/d0dc5fd1d568c66c.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Figure 1 - Digital signature migration\" width=\"2820\" height=\"870\">\n   </p><p>Figure 1 – Digital signature migration</p><p><b>Post-quantum readiness: </b>When creating new signing keys in AWS KMS as part of this migration, consider adopting post-quantum cryptography (PQC). AWS KMS supports ML-DSA (FIPS 204), so you can begin issuing PQC signatures now rather than as a separate future effort. This matters most for long-lived signatures, such as document signing, code signing, and certificate issuance.</p><p><b>Durability of imported asymmetric keys:</b> Asymmetric key material imported into AWS KMS (using <code>ImportKeyMaterial</code>) doesn’t have the same durability guarantees as keys generated within AWS KMS. In the unlikely event of an AWS Region-wide infrastructure failure, imported key material could become unavailable. To protect against this, create the key as a multi-Region key, then import the same key material into each replica key in other Regions. Also keep secure backups of imported private keys outside of AWS KMS. An AWS CloudHSM cluster backup is a zero-cost option; CloudHSM stores backups in <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3\">Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)</a></span> at no additional charge. For keys created in AWS KMS (not imported), AWS KMS automatically manages durability within a Region, and multi-Region keys replicate the key material for you.</p><h3 id=\"sec-4\">Encryption workloads</h3><p>For encryption workloads, use the gradual re-encryption pattern: encrypt new data with AWS KMS while a background process re-encrypts existing data created under AWS CloudHSM keys. This achieves a zero-downtime migration. The following are two approaches to gradual re-encryption, depending on your requirements.</p><p><b>Option A: </b>Direct encryption with AWS KMS API (recommended)</p><p>Call the AWS KMS <code>Encrypt</code> and <code>Decrypt</code> APIs directly (Figure 2). Your application passes plaintext to <code>kms:Encrypt</code> and receives a self-contained ciphertext blob. To decrypt, pass the blob to <code>kms:Decrypt</code>. There are no data keys to manage, no local cryptography, and no libraries beyond the AWS SDK. When decrypting, AWS KMS identifies the correct key from metadata it embeds in the ciphertext, including the key material identifier. As a result, ciphertext produced by AWS KMS is larger (by about 150 bytes) than AWS CloudHSM would produce for the same plaintext. During transition, your application must handle data encrypted by both systems. On decrypt, call <code>kms:Decrypt</code> first. If the ciphertext was created by <code>kms:Encrypt</code>, it will succeed. If it was created by CloudHSM, <code>kms:Decrypt</code> will return <code>InvalidCiphertextException</code> immediately without attempting decryption. Your application then falls back to the CloudHSM decrypt path and can optionally re-encrypt the plaintext with <code>kms:Encrypt</code> and store the new ciphertext. All new encrypt operations go to AWS KMS immediately; only decrypt needs the fallback until re-encryption is complete.</p><p>The following pseudocode illustrates this pattern:</p><pre class=\"language-text\"><code class=\"language-text\">def encrypt_record(record_id, record):\n    response = kms.encrypt(KeyId=kms_key_arn, Plaintext=record)\n    store(record_id, ciphertext=response['CiphertextBlob'])\n\ndef decrypt_record(record_id):\n    stored = fetch(record_id)\n    try:\n        response = kms.decrypt(CiphertextBlob=stored.ciphertext)\n        return response['Plaintext']\n    except kms.exceptions.InvalidCiphertextException:\n        # Not a KMS ciphertext - fall back to CloudHSM\n        return cloudhsm_decrypt(stored.ciphertext)</code></pre><p>\n   <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/9e28b8d561ac5a33.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/e26a094f680ee91e.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/4d231d072aba2354.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/9e28b8d561ac5a33.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Figure 2 - Encryption migration with AWS KMS API\" width=\"2820\" height=\"930\">\n   </p><p>Figure 2 – Encryption migration with AWS KMS API</p><p>More about AWS KMS encryption</p><ul> \n   <li>AWS KMS direct encryption supports plaintext up to 4 KB per request. For larger payloads, use Option B (<span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/encryption-sdk/latest/developer-guide/introduction.html\">AWS Encryption SDK</a></span>), which handles envelope encryption for you and supports data of any size.</li> \n   <li>AWS KMS also supports asymmetric encryption with RSA keys for use cases such as encrypting data destined for a specific key pair holder. This post focuses on symmetric encryption because it covers the majority of AWS CloudHSM encryption workloads.</li> \n  </ul><p><b>Option B: </b>Envelope encryption with AWS Encryption SDK</p><p>For workloads that require envelope encryption, use the AWS Encryption SDK as shown in Figure 3. Your application calls <code>encrypt()</code> on plaintext and <code>decrypt()</code> on ciphertext. The SDK manages data key generation, local 256-bit AES-GCM encryption, and serialization automatically. Its portable ciphertext format works across multiple runtimes of the AWS Encryption SDK. You can also enable data key caching to reduce calls to AWS KMS. Use this approach when you share encrypted data across services, languages, or with third parties outside of AWS.</p><p>\n   <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/ef85c042adee7160.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/1e0aaa16fa7919dd.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/46cabe8ea1b8db40.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/ef85c042adee7160.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Figure 3 - Encryption migration with AWS Encryption SDK\" width=\"2820\" height=\"930\">\n   </p><p>Figure 3 – Encryption migration with AWS Encryption SDK</p><h2 id=\"sec-5\">Migration results</h2><p>A financial services company migrated their transaction data encryption from AWS CloudHSM to AWS KMS. Prior to migration, their workload ran 75 million cryptographic operations per month on three HSMs provisioned for high availability and the operational burden was 25 hours per month. After the migration, they no longer had to manage HSMs, which reduced their operational burden to near zero.</p><table> \n    <tbody> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Metric</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Before (AWS CloudHSM)</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">After (AWS KMS)</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Monthly cost</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">$3,456 (three HSMs)</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Approximately $250 (93% reduction)</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Operational burden</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">25 hours per month (patching, certificates, and monitoring)</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Near zero: no HSMs to manage</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Compliance</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">FIPS 140-3 Level 3</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">FIPS 140-3 Level 3</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Disaster recovery</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Multi-Availability Zone (AZ) HSMs that they provisioned, sized, and monitored</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Automatic multi-AZ availability. Optional multi-Region replication</td> \n     </tr> \n     <tr> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">Migration duration</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">–</td> \n      <td colspan=\"1\" rowspan=\"1\">10 weeks, zero downtime</td> \n     </tr> \n    </tbody> \n   </table><h2 id=\"sec-6\">Recommendations</h2><p>We recommend the following as you migrate from AWS CloudHSM to AWS KMS.</p><ul> \n   <li>Use AI-assisted code transformation tools to accelerate refactoring and reduce manual effort.</li> \n   <li>Run the complete migration in staging first, including re-encryption of representative data volumes.</li> \n   <li>Document specific rollback criteria. During gradual re-encryption, you can pause at any point while AWS CloudHSM continues to decrypt data that has not yet moved. Keep KMS keys enabled after a rollback, because data already re-encrypted under AWS KMS still needs them.</li> \n   <li>Design <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/iam\">AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)</a></span> policies, key policies, grants, and encryption context schemas before migration. Encryption context enables fine-grained audit trails not available in CloudHSM. See the <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/understanding-kms-entries.html\">AWS KMS Developer Guide</a></span> for a list of log entries.</li> \n   <li>Delete CloudHSM keys only after all data is re-encrypted and verified. Before deleting, create a CloudHSM cluster backup of any keys imported into AWS KMS and export the public key of any signing key you replaced. Maintain an audit trail of the decommissioning.</li> \n  </ul><h2 id=\"sec-7\">Next steps</h2><p>Start by auditing your current AWS CloudHSM usage: document your keys, the applications that use them, your monthly operation volume, and any compliance constraints. Use the <span><a href=\"https://calculator.aws/\"><u>AWS Pricing Calculator</u></a></span> to estimate AWS KMS costs for your workload, then test a proof-of-concept migration with a non-critical application.</p><h2 id=\"sec-8\"><b>Conclusion</b></h2><p>Migrating from AWS CloudHSM to AWS KMS removes the recurring work of provisioning, scaling, patching, and monitoring HSM clusters, freeing your team to focus on your application rather than the cryptographic infrastructure beneath it. You gain built-in high availability across multiple Availability Zones, with optional multi-Region replication, at a usage-based cost rather than a per-hour commitment for hardware you manage. Because the migration involves refactoring application code, it is also a natural point to adopt post-quantum signing algorithms such as ML-DSA, so you modernize your security posture in the same effort.</p><p>For implementation support, contact your AWS account team or <span><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/professional-services/\"><u>AWS Professional Services</u></a></span>. For API integration details, see the <span><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/\"><u>AWS KMS Developer Guide</u></a></span>.</p><hr><p>If you have questions about this post, contact <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/contact-us/\">AWS Support</a> or start a new thread on <a href=\"https://repost.aws/\">AWS re:Post</a>.</p><p>\n   <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/452bc152e0ae7b4a.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Derek Tumulak\" width=\"120\" height=\"160\">\n  </p><h3 id=\"sec-9\">Derek Tumulak</h3><p>Derek Tumulak is a seasoned cybersecurity leader and Principal Product Manager at Amazon Web Services. With over 20 years of experience, he has held executive roles at Thales and Vormetric. A University of Waterloo graduate, Derek is a recognized expert in data security and encryption, frequently advising Silicon Valley organizations on advanced technical strategies and product innovation.</p>","zhKind","complete","蓝队","加固","加密","云原生","AWS","https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/reduce-operational-overhead-by-migrating-from-aws-cloudhsm-to-aws-kms/","AWS Security Blog",{"_124":125,"_126":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
